<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kortrijk</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Courtrai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kortrijk, in French Courtrai, is a city on the Lys (Leie) river in West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. A Roman settlement and a medieval centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a> and linen trade, it is known for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-golden-spurs">Battle of the Golden Spurs</a>, fought outside its walls in 1302, when Flemish militias defeated French knights. Its Broel towers and belfry recall its medieval importance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kortrijk.jpg" alt="Kortrijk" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: De Gunsch, Ann · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kortrijk_Grote_Markt_54_-_11083_-_onroerenderfgoed.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kortrijk lies on the river Leie, 42 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> and 25 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a>, and is the most populous city of its judicial and administrative arrondissement. The municipality includes the villages of Aalbeke, Bellegem, Bissegem, Heule, Kooigem, Marke and Rollegem, and it forms part of the cross-border Lille-Kortrijk-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tournai">Tournai</a> metropolitan area. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name, Cortoriacum, is taken to mean the settlement near the curb in the river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Cortoriacum was a larger Gallo-Roman vicus in the civitas Menapiorum, at a crossroads near the Leie where two Roman roads met. Remains of three Roman funeral pyres found in 1950 suggest that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> used it as an encampment during their invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 43 CE. In the 9th century Baldwin II, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, built fortifications there against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a>, and Philip, Count of Flanders, granted the town its charter in 1190. Joan, Countess of Flanders, exempted settlers from property tax to encourage weaving, and the town became a centre of linen production.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a> annexed the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> in 1300, he ordered a castle built in Kortrijk for a French garrison. Heavy taxes and the harsh response of the French governor Jacques de Châtillon led to open revolt in 1302. The town militia massacred his troops in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matins-of-bruges">Bruges Matins</a> on 18 May, and Flemish forces under Guy of Namur besieged the castle. On 11 July 1302 Count Robert II of Artois chose to fight in the open field, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-golden-spurs">Battle of the Golden Spurs</a> ended in an unexpected Flemish victory. The Flemish community commemorates the date as a holiday.</p><p>The 15th century was prosperous under the Dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, until the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a> in 1482 brought renewed fighting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> the French occupied the town five times in sixty years and razed its fortifications. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a> industry prospered again after the Napoleonic era, and the Treaty of Kortrijk of 1820 fixed the border between France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. The city was heavily bombed in 1917, and on 21 July 1944 about 300 Avro Lancasters dropped over 5,000 bombs on the centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kortrijk has about 80,000 inhabitants and a textile industry that still draws on its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a> tradition. Much of its medieval architecture survives, including the Broel Towers on the Leie, the late-Gothic city hall and the Church of Our Lady, where the golden spurs taken from the battlefield in 1302 were hung. The belfry and the béguinage are both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kortrijk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…1300, the townsmen of Bruges rose in the Bruges Matins of May 1302, and on 11 July 1302 an army of Flemish militias, fighting on foot with pikes and the spiked club called the goedendag, destroyed the French cavalry at the Battle of the Golden Spurs near <b>Courtrai</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>South of Lille, the army of Philip IV of France fought the Flemish militias that had defeated the French at <b>Kortrijk</b> two years earlier.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-18">18 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Flemish townsmen on foot routed the French cavalry at <b>Courtrai</b>, collecting hundreds of golden spurs from the dead.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-11">11 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Count Robert II was killed at <b>Courtrai</b> in 1302, and his daughter Mahaut defended her inheritance against her nephew Robert III.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-artois">County of Artois</a></li></ul></section>
